{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdgwUp8S1RbZkssaQACr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written candidate 5, `capacity_blur_personal_antisymmetry`, and it passes the self-check with `OK`. That check doesn't cover novelty against the other models, convergence, or how long the full fit takes, so admission still decides those.\n\n**Which model I refined and what changed:** I started from `personal_memory_capacity_blur`, which is statistically tied with the best model (0.8 ± 6.1 nats behind). Its mechanism stays as it was: each person has their own working-memory capacity, so their judgement is sharp on short pairs and blurred on pairs near or beyond that capacity. The one change is a part taken from the incumbent: how strongly a person rejects antisymmetric sequences (ones that equal their own reverse with heads and tails swapped, like HHTT or HTHTHTHT) is now a personal weight drawn from a population, not one shared weight.\n\n**Why this change:** the critique's one discrepancy that survives FDR is that people differ in how often they agree with the majority more than the incumbent produces (q = 0.032). This model now has two personal sources of that variation. Memory capacity mostly affects long pairs, and the antisymmetry weight mostly affects pairs with an antisymmetric sequence, so they should be told apart rather than overlap.\n\n**Self-check result:** the short single-chain smoke fit gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −3459.8. Don't compare that number with the full-fit scores in the model list, because this smoke fit isn't a reliable estimate. The check also warned that PSIS-LOO was unreliable: 10.1% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. A full fit could get the same unreliable-LOO flag, which would make this model's ELPD estimate untrustworthy in the comparison.\n\n**Risks:**\n- **Speed:** one chain of 100 tuning plus 100 draws took 128 seconds. The full fit has to finis